</tbody></table>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="25" id="mwBwc" aria-labelledby="Channels_and_release_schedule"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Channels_and_release_schedule">Channels and release schedule</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Firefox&amp;action=edit&amp;section=25" title="Edit section: Channels and release schedule"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwBwg">In March 2011, Mozilla presented plans to switch to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefox_Rapid_Release_Model" title="Firefox Rapid Release Model" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBwk">rapid release model</a>, a faster 16-week <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Development_cycle" title="Development cycle" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBwo">development cycle</a>, similar to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Chrome" title="Google Chrome" id="mwBws">Google Chrome</a>. <i id="mwBww"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ars_Technica" title="Ars Technica" id="mwBw0">Ars Technica</a></i> noted that this new cycle entailed "significant technical and operational challenges" for Mozilla (notably preserving third-party <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Add-on_(Mozilla)" title="Add-on (Mozilla)" id="mwBw4">add-on</a> compatibility), but that it would help accelerate Firefox's adoption of new web standards, features, and performance improvements.<sup about="#mwt1134" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PaulRyan_270-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PaulRyan"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-PaulRyan-270"}}'><a href="#cite_note-PaulRyan-270" id="mwBw8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxE">[</span>270<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1137" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-271" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-271"}}'><a href="#cite_note-271" id="mwBxM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxU">[</span>271<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxY">]</span></span></a></sup> This plan was implemented in April 2011.<sup about="#mwt1140" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-272" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-272"}}'><a href="#cite_note-272" id="mwBxc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxk">[</span>272<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxo">]</span></span></a></sup> The release process was split into four "channels", with major releases trickling down to the next channel every six to eight weeks. For example, the Nightly channel would feature a preliminary unstable version of Firefox 6, which would move to the experimental "Aurora" channel after preliminary testing, then to the more stable "beta" channel, before finally reaching the public release channel, with each stage taking around six weeks.<sup about="#mwt1143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-auto1_273-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"auto1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-auto1-273"}}'><a href="#cite_note-auto1-273" id="mwBxs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBx0">[</span>273<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBx4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1144" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PaulRyan_270-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PaulRyan"}}'><a href="#cite_note-PaulRyan-270" id="mwBx8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByE">[</span>270<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1147" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-274" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-274"}}'><a href="#cite_note-274" id="mwByM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByU">[</span>274<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByY">]</span></span></a></sup> For corporations, Mozilla introduced an Extended Support Release (ESR) channel, with new versions released every 30 weeks (and supported for 12 more weeks after a new ESR version is released), though Mozilla warned that it would be less secure than the release channel, since security patches would only be <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backporting" title="Backporting" id="mwByc">backported</a> for high-impact vulnerabilities.<sup about="#mwt1150" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-275" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-275"}}'><a href="#cite_note-275" id="mwByg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByo">[</span>275<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBys">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1153" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-276" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-276"}}'><a href="#cite_note-276" id="mwByw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBy0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBy4">[</span>276<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBy8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBzA">In 2017, Mozilla abandoned the Aurora channel, which saw low uptake, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rebasing" title="Rebasing" id="mwBzE">rebased</a> Firefox Developer Edition onto the beta channel.<sup about="#mwt1156" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-277" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-277"}}'><a href="#cite_note-277" id="mwBzI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzQ">[</span>277<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzU">]</span></span></a></sup> Mozilla uses <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A/B_testing" title="A/B testing" id="mwBzY">A/B testing</a><sup about="#mwt1159" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-278" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-278"}}'><a href="#cite_note-278" id="mwBzc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzk">[</span>278<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzo">]</span></span></a></sup> and a staged rollout mechanism for the release channel, where updates are first presented to a small fraction of users, with Mozilla monitoring its telemetry for increased crashes or other issues before the update is made available to all users.<sup about="#mwt1160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-auto1_273-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"auto1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-auto1-273" id="mwBzs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBz0">[</span>273<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBz4">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2020, Firefox moved to a four-week release cycle, to catch up with Chrome in support for new web features.<sup about="#mwt1163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-279" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-279"}}'><a href="#cite_note-279" id="mwBz8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0E">[</span>279<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-280" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-280"}}'><a href="#cite_note-280" id="mwB0M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0U">[</span>280<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0Y">]</span></span></a></sup> Chrome switched to a four-week cycle a year later.<sup about="#mwt1169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-281" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-281"}}'><a href="#cite_note-281" id="mwB0c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0k">[</span>281<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0o">]</span></span></a></sup> Starting on September 1, 2026, Firefox will move to its two-week cycle.<sup about="#mwt1172" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-282" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-282"}}'><a href="#cite_note-282" id="mwB0s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB00">[</span>282<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB04">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="26" id="mwB08" aria-labelledby="Licensing"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Licensing">Licensing</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Firefox&amp;action=edit&amp;section=26" title="Edit section: Licensing"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwB1A">Firefox <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source_code" title="Source code" id="mwB1E">source code</a> is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software" title="Free software" id="mwB1I">free software</a>, with most of it being released under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mozilla_Public_License" title="Mozilla Public License" id="mwB1M">Mozilla Public License</a> (MPL) version 2.0.<sup about="#mwt1175" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Licensing-Policies_11-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Licensing-Policies"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Licensing-Policies-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Licensing-Policies-11" id="mwB1Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB1U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1Y">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1c">]</span></span></a></sup> This license permits anyone to view, modify, or redistribute the source code. As a result, several publicly released applications have been built from it, including Firefox's predecessor <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netscape_(web_browser)" title="Netscape (web browser)" id="mwB1g">Netscape</a>,<sup about="#mwt1178" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-283" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-283"}}'><a href="#cite_note-283" id="mwB1k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB1o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1s">[</span>283<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1w">]</span></span></a></sup> the customizable <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pale_Moon" title="Pale Moon" id="mwB10">Pale Moon</a>, and the privacy focused <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tor_Browser" title="Tor Browser" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB14">Tor Browser</a>.<sup about="#mwt1181" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-284" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-284"}}'><a href="#cite_note-284" id="mwB18"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB2A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2E">[</span>284<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2I">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB2M">In the past, Firefox was licensed solely under the MPL, then version 1.1,<sup about="#mwt1184" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-mozrelicensing_285-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"mozrelicensing"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-mozrelicensing-285"}}'><a href="#cite_note-mozrelicensing-285" id="mwB2Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB2U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2Y">[</span>285<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2c">]</span></span></a></sup> which the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_Software_Foundation" title="Free Software Foundation" id="mwB2g">Free Software Foundation</a> criticized for being <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weak_copyleft" title="Weak copyleft" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB2k">weak copyleft</a>, as the license permitted, in limited ways, proprietary <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derivative_work" title="Derivative work" id="mwB2o">derivative works</a>. Additionally, code only licensed under MPL 1.1 could not legally be linked with code under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPL" title="GPL" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB2s">GPL</a>.<sup about="#mwt1187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-286" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-286"}}'><a href="#cite_note-286" id="mwB2w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB20"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB24">[</span>286<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB28">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1190" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-287" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-287"}}'><a href="#cite_note-287" id="mwB3A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3I">[</span>287<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3M">]</span></span></a></sup> To address these concerns, Mozilla re-licensed most of Firefox under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tri-license" title="Tri-license" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB3Q">tri-license</a> scheme of MPL 1.1, GPL 2.0, or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGPL" title="LGPL" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB3U">LGPL</a> 2.1. Since the re-licensing, developers were free to choose the license under which they received most of the code, to suit their intended use: GPL or LGPL linking and derivative works when one of those licenses is chosen, or MPL use (including the possibility of proprietary derivative works) if they chose the MPL.<sup about="#mwt1191" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-mozrelicensing_285-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"mozrelicensing"}}'><a href="#cite_note-mozrelicensing-285" id="mwB3Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3g">[</span>285<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3k">]</span></span></a></sup> However, Mozilla released the GPL-compatible MPL 2.0<sup about="#mwt1194" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-288" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-288"}}'><a href="#cite_note-288" id="mwB3o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3w">[</span>288<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB30">]</span></span></a></sup> on January 3, 2012,<sup about="#mwt1197" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-289" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-289"}}'><a href="#cite_note-289" id="mwB34"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB38"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4A">[</span>289<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4E">]</span></span></a></sup> and with the release of Firefox 13 on June 5, 2012, Mozilla used it to replace the tri-licensing scheme.<sup about="#mwt1200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-290" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-290"}}'><a href="#cite_note-290" id="mwB4I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4Q">[</span>290<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4U">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="27" id="mwB4Y" aria-labelledby="Logo_and_visual_identity"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Logo_and_visual_identity">Logo and visual identity</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Firefox&amp;action=edit&amp;section=27" title="Edit section: Logo and visual identity"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1201" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB4c" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Firefox logo"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1202" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1201" id="mwB4g">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefox_logo" title="Firefox logo">Firefox logo</a></div>

<p id="mwB4k">The Firefox icon is a trademark used to designate the official Mozilla build of the Firefox software and builds of official distribution partners.<sup about="#mwt1204" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-291" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-291"}}'><a href="#cite_note-291" id="mwB4o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4w">[</span>291<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB40">]</span></span></a></sup> For this reason, software distributors who distribute modified versions of Firefox do not use the icon.<sup about="#mwt1205" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Mozilla_Trademark_Guidelines_292-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Mozilla Trademark Guidelines"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Mozilla_Trademark_Guidelines-292" id="mwB44"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB48"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5A">[</span>292<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB5I">Early Firebird and Phoenix releases of Firefox were considered to have reasonable visual designs but fell short when compared to many other professional software packages. In October 2003, professional interface designer Steven Garrity authored an article covering everything he considered to be wrong with Mozilla's visual identity.<sup about="#mwt1208" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-branding-mozilla_293-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"branding-mozilla"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-branding-mozilla-293"}}'><a href="#cite_note-branding-mozilla-293" id="mwB5M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5U">[</span>293<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB5c">Shortly afterwards, the Mozilla Foundation invited Garrity to head up the new visual identity team. The release of Firefox 0.8 in February 2004 saw the introduction of the new branding efforts. Included were new icon designs by silverorange, a group of web developers with a long-standing relationship with Mozilla. The final renderings are by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jon_Hicks_(designer)" title="Jon Hicks (designer)" id="mwB5g">Jon Hicks</a>, who had worked on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camino_(web_browser)" title="Camino (web browser)" id="mwB5k">Camino</a>.<sup about="#mwt1211" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-branding-mozilla-followup_294-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"branding-mozilla-followup"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-branding-mozilla-followup-294"}}'><a href="#cite_note-branding-mozilla-followup-294" id="mwB5o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5w">[</span>294<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB50">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1214" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-branding-firefox_295-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"branding-firefox"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-branding-firefox-295"}}'><a href="#cite_note-branding-firefox-295" id="mwB54"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB58"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6A">[</span>295<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6E">]</span></span></a></sup> The logo was later revised and updated, fixing several flaws found when it was enlarged.<sup about="#mwt1217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-spot-the-difference_296-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"spot-the-difference"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-spot-the-difference-296"}}'><a href="#cite_note-spot-the-difference-296" id="mwB6I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6Q">[</span>296<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6U">]</span></span></a></sup> The animal shown in the logo is a stylized fox, although "firefox" is usually a common name for the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_panda" title="Red panda" id="mwB6Y">red panda</a>. The panda, according to Hicks, "didn't really conjure up the right imagery" and was not widely known.<sup about="#mwt1218" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-branding-firefox_295-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"branding-firefox"}}'><a href="#cite_note-branding-firefox-295" id="mwB6c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6k">[</span>295<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6o">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB6s">In 2017, Mozilla made a sleeker, newer design of the Firefox logo as part of the quantum update.<sup about="#mwt1221" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-297" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-297"}}'><a href="#cite_note-297" id="mwB6w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB60"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB64">[</span>297<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB68">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB7A">In June 2019, Mozilla unveiled a revised Firefox logo, which was officially implemented on version 70. The new logo is part of an effort to build a brand system around Firefox and its complementary apps and services, which are now being promoted as a suite under the Firefox brand.<sup about="#mwt1224" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-298" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-298"}}'><a href="#cite_note-298" id="mwB7E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7M">[</span>298<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB7U"><span class="anchor" id="Kit_(mascot)" about="#mwt1225" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kit (mascot)"}},"i":0}}]}'></span>
In March 2026, Mozilla gave a name to the fox mascot, now named Kit.<sup about="#mwt1228" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:5_299-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":5"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:5-299"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:5-299" id="mwB7Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7g">[</span>299<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7k">]</span></span></a></sup> Kit now mostly appears independent from the globe and throughout Firefox's branding and the product itself, such as error screens and settings pages.<sup about="#mwt1231" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-300" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-300"}}'><a href="#cite_note-300" id="mwB7o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7w">[</span>300<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB70">]</span></span></a></sup> Kit was designed to feel "like your companion for this internet era", referring mostly to the proliferation of AI content online.<sup about="#mwt1234" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-301" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-301"}}'><a href="#cite_note-301" id="mwB74"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB78"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8A">[</span>301<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB8I">Alongside Kit, Firefox was given a new brand identity, with a new font and colors similar to the globe from the logo.<sup about="#mwt1235" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:5_299-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:5-299" id="mwB8M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB8Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8U">[</span>299<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<ul class="gallery mw-gallery-traditional" style="text-align:center" typeof="mw:Extension/gallery" about="#mwt1239" id="mwB8c" data-mw='{"name":"gallery","attrs":{"style":"text-align:center"},"body":{}}'>
